// Practice quiz: Calling your Spring Boot API from the browser
// First draft: unreviewed. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: html-css-js/fetch-and-browser-client/calling-your-spring-boot-api-from-the-browser

::o1 default method::What method does fetch(url) use with no options? {
~POST #Not quite. POST must be set explicitly.
=GET #Correct. GET is the default.
~OPTIONS #Not quite. That is used for preflights, by the browser.
~HEAD #Not quite. That must be set explicitly.
}

::o1 string body type::A POST has a string body and no Content-Type header. What does fetch send as the type? {
~application/json #Not quite. It does not inspect the text.
~application/octet-stream #Not quite. That is not the string default.
=text/plain;charset\=UTF-8 #Correct. A string body defaults to this, so a JSON endpoint may answer 415.
~No header at all, ever #Not quite. fetch adds the default.
}

::o1 serializing::What converts a JavaScript object into a body for a JSON POST? {
=JSON.stringify(data) #Correct. It produces the JSON string to send.
~JSON.parse(data) #Not quite. That goes the other way.
~String(data) #Not quite. That produces the text [object Object].
~fetch does it automatically #Not quite. It does not serialize plain objects.
}

::o2 which value to show::After a successful create, what should the page show? {
~The input field text, always #Not quite. The server may have changed or added values.
~Nothing, until a manual refresh #Not quite. That hides the result.
~The raw response headers #Not quite. Those are not meant for users.
=The record returned by the server #Correct. It reflects what was stored, including any id.
}

::o2 mapping 415::Your own client gets a 415. Who should act on it? {
=The developer, because the client code sent the wrong content type #Correct. A user cannot fix a wrong header, so show a generic message and fix the code.
~The user, by retyping the form #Not quite. The form content is not the cause.
~The user, by signing in again #Not quite. Authentication is unrelated.
~Nobody, because it resolves by itself #Not quite. The same request will keep failing.
}
